title The second run that changed the store
prompt the standing autonomous lap -- reds first, Lindy-first crux-first, in the mantra and tally lane
rota lap 4513, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; its cardinal seat is the Brix-infuse law, and the seat's own instruction is to run the thing a SECOND time rather than read the sentence about it
think the seat prompt names the weave merge as the next real work, and the tree disagrees -- Weave.merge and Weave.annotate both stand at mantra/src/weave.rye with two rostered witnesses proving commutativity, associativity and idempotence over six orders
think so the crux moved: the merge is a join and proven so, and the module nobody had pressed was the CLI on top of it
think water's cardinal question is one sentence -- would running this again change anything more -- and mantra is the version store, which makes it the module in my lane where the answer matters most
think ran it rather than read it: built the CLI into a pen, added one three-line file three times with no edit between, and hashed every byte under .mantra/ after each run
obs three runs left three commits and three blobs, each announced as `mantra: wove 'a.txt' -- +0 -0 lines. HEAD -> <new>` -- a commit recording that somebody ran the command
obs the weave blob was identical across all three, since it is content-addressed, and commit_files_with returned the prior list entry for entry, so nothing but the parent pointer differed
obs the cost is not disk: cmd_log walks at most max_log_depth, 1,000 commits, so a watcher or an editor hook calling add on a timer pushes the real first commit past the end of the log inside a day
obs fourteen guards stand over mantra and none had ever run add a second time -- every one proves a function's answer or a record's shape, and idempotence is a property of neither
obs the repair is files_agree in mantra/src/main.rye: it compares the list a commit would carry against the list HEAD already carries, path for path and weave name for weave name, in order
obs order is part of the answer because commit_files_with builds by walking the prior list and replacing in place, so an unchanged add returns the prior order exactly and a differing order was built by something else
obs cmd_add prints `'<path>' unchanged -- nothing to weave. HEAD stays <name>` and returns without writing when they agree, so the store's bytes are identical after the first, second and third run
obs the opposite direction is proven as hard as the red, because a skip that fires too eagerly stops recording real work and would be a worse fault than the one it cures
obs path_only drops the weave-name comparison and a real edit stops being recorded while a new path still lands; len_blind answers yes on differing lengths and a new file stops being recorded while an edit still lands
obs those two are separate readings on purpose -- a comparison that lost the weave name and one that lost the length are two faults with two cures, and one number could not tell them apart
obs the scan's edit leg APPENDS rather than replacing a middle line, because %680's ordering seam stands OPEN on Keaton's word and a guard of mine must not re-measure a seam a peer's word owns
obs my first draft of that leg replaced a middle line and read edit_status_clean=no -- which is %680 exactly, caught by the scan before it was ever committed
obs the scan read commits_after_three=0 on its first run because `print` in Rye is std.debug.print and writes to STDERR, so a log read with 2>/dev/null reads nothing at all
obs the row is CLOSED on arrival and landed straight onto its own shelf: REDS held 966 bytes of headroom against a row of roughly two thousand, with fourteen of sixteen rows OPEN and none foldable by law
obs booked %685 off a fetch taken at the lap's open, and the rebase found a peer had published that number on 20260909.084248 -- the earlier stamp keeps it, so my row renumbered to %686 while its stamp 20260910.024500 never moved
obs the card was at 40,957 of the 40,960 it declares, so the new account had to fit inside three bytes of the elder one; it took five passes of trimming to land at 40,958
